    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_groups",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Three groups",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Classification",
        "lines": [
          "The stem is modified for storage of food,",
          "support, protection, photosynthesis and",
          "vegetative propagation.",
          {
            "text": "Three groups: underground, aerial and",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "sub-aerial stem modifications."
        ],
        "why": "The 8-mark answer is organised by WHERE the modified stem lives - under the soil, in the air, or running along the ground. Writing this frame first keeps the nine examples sorted.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Jumping straight to examples with no classification line - the organisation itself carries a mark."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Under, air, along the ground - the three places a stem can go.",
        "margin_note": "One framing line, then the three headed lists below."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_underground",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Underground stems",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Four types",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "A. Underground stem modifications:",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "1. Rhizome: grows horizontally at a certain",
          "depth in the soil. Ex: ginger, turmeric.",
          "2. Corm: grows vertically in the soil.",
          "Ex: Colocasia, Amorphophallus.",
          "3. Stem tuber: the tip of an underground",
          "branch swells with stored food; buds sit in",
          "the eyes. Ex: potato.",
          "4. Bulb: the stem is reduced to a disc and",
          "fleshy leaf bases store the food. Ex: onion.",
          "All of them store food and also propagate",
          "the plant vegetatively."
        ],
        "why": "The four underground types are separated by one line each: rhizome grows horizontally, corm vertically, a tuber is a swollen branch TIP, and in a bulb the LEAF BASES (not the stem) hold the food.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Calling potato a root. The eyes are buds - only a stem carries buds.",
          "Writing that the bulb stem stores food. In a bulb the stem is a small disc; the fleshy leaf bases store the food.",
          "Swapping rhizome (horizontal) and corm (vertical)."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Ginger lies flat (rhizome), Amorphophallus stands (corm), potato is a swollen tip (tuber), onion is all leaf bases (bulb).",
        "margin_note": "Four names, one direction or storage fact each, one example each."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_aerial",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Aerial modifications",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Three types",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "B. Aerial stem modifications:",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "1. Stem tendril: an axillary or terminal bud",
          "becomes a slender, spirally coiled structure",
          "used for climbing.",
          "From axillary buds: cucumber, pumpkin,",
          "watermelon. From a terminal bud: grapevine.",
          "2. Thorn: a hard, woody, straight and pointed",
          "structure; it protects the plant from grazing",
          "animals. Ex: Bougainvillea, Citrus.",
          "3. Phylloclade: in xerophytes the stem becomes",
          "green and flattened or cylindrical and does",
          "photosynthesis. Ex: Opuntia, Casuarina."
        ],
        "why": "Each aerial type is a BUD or branch rebuilt for one job: tendril = climbing, thorn = protection, phylloclade = photosynthesis in dry habitats. The bud origin (axillary vs terminal) is the detail examiners reward in tendrils.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Calling the phylloclade a leaf. It is the stem; in Opuntia the leaves are the spines.",
          "Writing curved for thorn. A stem thorn is straight and pointed; a prickle is curved."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Climb, defend, cook: tendril climbs, thorn defends, phylloclade makes the food.",
        "margin_note": "Grapevine (terminal bud) vs cucumber (axillary bud) is the pair worth writing."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_subaerial",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Sub-aerial modifications",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Three types",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "C. Sub-aerial stem modifications:",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "1. Runner: a branch that grows flat along the",
          "ground and roots at the nodes; it spreads the",
          "plant to new places.",
          "Ex: grasses, strawberry, Oxalis.",
          "2. Stolon: a slender lateral branch from the",
          "base of the main axis; it grows up for some",
          "time, bends down to touch the ground and",
          "produces adventitious roots.",
          "Ex: jasmine, Nerium.",
          "3. Sucker: an underground lateral branch that",
          "grows horizontally and then comes out",
          "obliquely upward. Ex: Chrysanthemum."
        ],
        "why": "All three spread the plant near the parent; they differ by path. Runner: along the ground. Stolon: starts in air, bends down. Sucker: starts underground, comes up. The path is what the examiner reads for.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Swapping stolon and sucker. Stolon starts in the AIR and bends down; sucker starts UNDER the soil and comes up.",
          "Writing that a runner grows underground. It runs on the surface, rooting at nodes."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Runner runs on the ground, stolon stoops down to it, sucker surfaces from below it.",
        "margin_note": "State each path (on, down to, up from the ground) - the example alone is not enough."
      },
